{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1476 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6d0d2d94ed1124460746ce82a6a7f3a7d4cd89f8ad4588884acd69372f5fef87" :edb/db-before-t 1475 :edb/db-after-t 1476 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534788 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:54:59.702Z" 13194139534788 true] [17592186105530 1100 "Evaluate  $\\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x .$" 13194139534788 true] [17592186105530 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534788 false] [17592186105530 1145 "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x &= \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\int_{a}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] & = \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left(\\arctan (x) \\Big \\mid _a^0 \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left(0 \\right) - \\arctan \\left(a \\right) \\right] \\\\[5pt] & = 0 - \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\arctan \\left(a \\right) \\\\[5pt] &=-\\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function (i.e., it is symmetrical about the $y$-axis). This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^\\infty \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+ \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534788 false] [17592186105530 1145 "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x  \n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t&= \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\int_{a}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\t\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t& =  \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left( \\arctan (x) \\Big|_a^0 \\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n \t \t\t\t& =  \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left( 0 \\right) -  \\arctan \\left( a \\right) \\right] \\[5pt]\n\n \t\t\t        & =  0 - \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}  \\arctan \\left( a \\right) \\[5pt]\n\n                                &= - \\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\n \t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function (i.e., it is symmetrical about the $y$-axis). This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^\\infty \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x  =\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x &=   \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x +  \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+ \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\pi.}\n$$" 13194139534788 true] [17592186105531 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = e^{-x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is convergent\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent" 13194139534788 false] [17592186105531 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = e^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is convergent\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent" 13194139534788 true] [17592186105531 1145 "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$, then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results.\n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our integrals.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement I, we get\n$\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\int_{0}^{a} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &= \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\left[-e^{-x}\\right]_0^a \\\\ &= -\\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\left({e^{-a}}-1\\right) \\\\ &= 1. \\end{aligned}$\nSo the integral converges, and statement I is true.\n- Computing the integral in statement II, we get\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{- \\infty}^{0}e^{-x}dx= \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}\\int_{a}^{0}e^{-x}dx = \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}[- e^{-x}]_{a}^{0} = \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}(e^{-a} - 1) = + \\infty$.\nSo the integral diverges, and statement II is true.\n- The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx= \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$.\nHowever, the first integral on the right-hand side is divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are all true." 13194139534788 false] [17592186105531 1145 "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our integrals.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement I, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n&=  \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\int_{0}^{a} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\n&=  \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\left[-e^{-x}\\right]_0^a\\\n&=  -\\lim_{a \\to \\infty}  \\left({e^{-a}}-1\\right)\\\n&=  1.\n}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges, and statement I is true.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{e}^{-x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-a}-1) \\\\  & =+\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral diverges, and statement II is true.\n\n- The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx= \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nHowever, the first integral on the right-hand side is divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are all true." 13194139534788 true] [17592186105532 1145 "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/6c86b60d83c741287c2b7770bfddd67fe9dcfece5ba255edf8ebdb8f0360a975.png)\n\nIn this case, we must calculate the improper integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs usual, we break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x  + \\displaystyle  \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x  & =  9\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left( \\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\arctan \\left(2x \\right) \\right]_a^0 \\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n \t \t\t\t                                                   & = \\dfrac{9}{2} \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left( 0 \\right) -  \\arctan \\left( 2a \\right) \\right]  \\[5pt] \n\n \t\t\t                                                           & =  0 - \\dfrac{9}{2}\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}  \\arctan \\left( 2a \\right) \\[5pt] \n\n \t\t\t                                                           & = -\\dfrac{9}{2} \\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\n \t\t\t\t                                                   & = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}.\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. Therefore, we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^\\infty\\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x =  \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}+ \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}= \\dfrac{9\\pi}{2} .\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the required area is equal to $\\dfrac{9\\pi}{2}$ square units." 13194139534788 true] [17592186105532 1145 "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1382/Source/Images/12de9a29b2f0e4eaad17166bc9fc3b49.png)\n\nIn this case, we must calculate the improper integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nAs usual, we break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x & = 9\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left(\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\arctan \\left(2x \\right) \\right]_a^0 \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{9}{2} \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left(0 \\right) - \\arctan \\left(2a \\right) \\right] \\\\[5pt] & = 0 - \\dfrac{9}{2}\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\arctan \\left(2a \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = -\\dfrac{9}{2} \\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. Therefore, we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^\\infty\\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}+ \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}= \\dfrac{9\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the required area is equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nsquare units." 13194139534788 false] [17592186105847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310232 13194139534788 true] [17592186105847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310239 13194139534788 true] [17592186105847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310267 13194139534788 true] [17592186105847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310297 13194139534788 true] [17592186105848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310246 13194139534788 true] [17592186105848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310260 13194139534788 true] [17592186105848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310274 13194139534788 true] [17592186105848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310277 13194139534788 true] [17592186105848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310281 13194139534788 true] [17592186105849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310253 13194139534788 true] [17592186105849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310284 13194139534788 true] [17592186105849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310287 13194139534788 true] [17592186105849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310290 13194139534788 true] [17592186106306 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534788 true] [17592186106306 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = 3x^{2}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty}f(x)dx$ is convergent\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{- \\infty}^{0}f(x)dx$ is divergent\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{- \\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)dx$ is divergent" 13194139534788 false] [17592186106306 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" 13194139534788 true] [17592186106306 1145 "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({0}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to -\\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true." 13194139534788 true] [17592186106421 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534788 true] [17592186106421 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/478ea8a880f959248ae2885776ca3c2b3774935f96ddb64ce531adc6fc5b3ef7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." 13194139534788 true] [17592186106421 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1382/Source/Images/q-81168.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y = \\frac{1}{9 + x^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." 13194139534788 false] [17592186106421 1145 "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{3}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310232 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310233 13194139534788 true] [17592186310232 1089 "q-118755" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310232 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534788 true] [17592186310232 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310232 1113 #uuid "9731bfc9-4d0e-5e7d-ad16-7fdb4db34101" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310232 1145 "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true." 13194139534788 true] [17592186310233 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310234 13194139534788 true] [17592186310233 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310235 13194139534788 true] [17592186310233 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310236 13194139534788 true] [17592186310233 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310237 13194139534788 true] [17592186310233 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310238 13194139534788 true] [17592186310233 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534788 true] [17592186310233 1041 "selection" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310233 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310235 13194139534788 true] [17592186310233 1110 #uuid "14053664-c185-5448-ab52-b588172e5b3a" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310234 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310234 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534788 true] [17592186310234 1111 #uuid "d9525ff3-b890-517b-8dad-627b0bf61f44" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310235 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534788 true] [1759