calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{-{x}^{4}}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310261 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310262 13194139534788 true] [17592186310261 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310263 13194139534788 true] [17592186310261 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310264 13194139534788 true] [17592186310261 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310265 13194139534788 true] [17592186310261 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310266 13194139534788 true] [17592186310261 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534788 true] [17592186310261 1041 "selection" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310261 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310266 13194139534788 true] [17592186310261 1110 #uuid "d0e62e8d-16b3-5e7f-b976-02a12c5613da" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310262 1034 "3" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310262 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534788 true] [17592186310262 1111 #uuid "e49169e1-b