e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310283 13194139534788 true] [17592186310282 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534788 true] [17592186310282 1041 "field-1" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310282 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310283 13194139534788 true] [17592186310282 1110 #uuid "f87090dc-26b0-5918-ac81-25a73de8940d" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310283 1034 "3" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534788 true] [17592186310283 1111 #uuid "d7f7daef-d6f6-50cb-bf96-f325761c3d1a" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310285 13194139534788 true] [17592186310284 1089 "q-351595" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310284 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534788 true] [17592186310284 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037