n}⁡{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310289 13194139534788 true] [17592186310288 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534788 true] [17592186310288 1041 "field-1" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310288 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310289 13194139534788 true] [17592186310288 1110 #uuid "ba779321-fc86-57fb-be6e-6ca842951e91" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310289 1034 "2\\pi" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534788 true] [17592186310289 1111 #uuid "cfd690c3-c823-518a-8ea9-bd14623d30c0" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310290 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310291 13194139534788 true] [17592186310290 1089 "q-6302" 13194139534788 true] [17592186310290 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534788 true] [17592186310290 1100 "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}$ and the $x$-axis." 13194139534788 true] [17592186310290 1113 #uuid "7f18d536-32a1-5975-855f-f52f46d77aa0" 13194